  "summary_text": "Kyle, Port Washington is outperforming the DOW-adjusted expectation by 4.9% month-to-date, which is the win to own\u2014the calendar mix was unfavorable this period, but the store executed better than expected. That said, yesterday's grooming revenue at Port dropped 9% week-over-week, driven by a capacity miss: only 6 active groomers produced $438 per groomer, 11% below your trailing 30-day baseline of $494. The good news is add-on attach climbed to 61% from 52%, and retail jumped 42%, so front-of-house discipline is solid. Today and tomorrow you have 8 and 8 grooming slots scheduled respectively, which should recover that productivity gap. Hicksville told a different story yesterday\u2014it posted a 94% revenue jump on 160% more transactions, with core grooming revenue nearly doubling week-over-week, though that's off a very low base (2 grooming appts the prior Monday). Month-to-date Hicksville is down 10% versus last September, but again that's ahead of the DOW-adjusted expectation by 6%, so real performance is positive. The constraint is capacity: only 2 active groomers yesterday, and you have just 22 groomer-days scheduled for the next seven days versus Port's 45. Both stores carry a combined lapse pool of 709 customers worth $457,582 annually\u2014that's your reactivation opportunity. Glen Cove remains in setup mode with zero production.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Port Washington has 8 grooming slots today and 8 tomorrow with 15 bather hours tomorrow; Hicksville has 3 today and 4 tomorrow with zero bather coverage; next seven days Port is 45 groomer-days and Hicksville is 22 groomer-days."
